From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Sat, 11 Oct 2026 00:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Say what RLD entry was skipped, and skip only it

An RLD entry this reader cannot turn into a relocation used to end the
walk of its block: the length of a complex expression (entry type 017)
is not in the size table, so the loop hit its `arg < 0' guard and
broke, with nothing said.  Everything after that entry in the same
block was lost with it, and a complex expression is far from the only
thing that followed.

Measured on a module whose complex entry sits between ordinary ones:
of the four entries dumpobj lists, this reader built one, and the
program read 010 and 012 out of a table where the two relocated
addresses belonged.  Worse, an entry can also switch the section the
following entries apply to (type 007), and when that switch is the
entry that got dropped, the relocations after it land on the *previous*
section: a two-psect module had BCODE's two relocations attributed to
ACODE, which is two bytes long, one of them past its end.  No
diagnostic in either case.

So: step over the entry instead of giving up on the block, and say what
was skipped and where.

Stepping over means knowing the length, which for a complex expression
means walking its string of operation codes -- table 2-7 of the RT-11
Volume and File Formats Manual: codes 0 through 011 carry no operand,
016 carries four bytes of RADIX-50 name, 017 a section number and
offset, 020 a constant, and the string ends with a store, 012 or 013.
obj_pdp11_complex_size() does that and reports -1 for a string that
does not end inside its block; the two cases that remain genuinely
unknowable -- an entry type the manual does not define, and an entry
running off the end of its block -- still stop the walk, but now with a
message naming the type.

Saying so is two halves.  The message itself names the kind and the
place (`CODE+0x2: a complex relocation expression (RLD entry type 017)
is not supported'), which is as much as a reader can offer.  And the
section carrying such an entry is marked, so that asking it for its
relocations fails: that is what every link does, so a link that would
have come out quietly wrong now fails outright, while the file stays
readable for everything else -- its symbols, its contents, its other
sections' relocations.  There is no third behaviour available in a BFD
backend; relocations here are built when the file is opened, and
failing *that* would only say "file format not recognized".

No ordinary object is affected.  A complex expression is what MACRO-11
emits for an expression that goes beyond + and - over a relocatable or
external symbol -- multiplication, division, the logical operators, a
complement, or two relocatable symbols in one expression -- and there
is not one in DEC's own SYSLIB.OBJ: 112 modules, 166 RLD blocks, 254
entries, of types 01, 02, 04, 07, 010, 015 and 016 only.  Verified that
the library still lists and reads as before, that a plain MACRO-11
module still links and runs on the machine (a .word table of addresses
comes out relocated), and that hello, math and fileio still pass.
---
 bfd/obj-pdp11.c | 149 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 146 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/bfd/obj-pdp11.c b/bfd/obj-pdp11.c
--- a/bfd/obj-pdp11.c	2026-10-11 00:30:00
+++ b/bfd/obj-pdp11.c	2026-10-11 00:30:00
@@ -134,6 +134,17 @@
 /* Set in the command byte when the relocation is of a byte, not a
    word.  */
 #define OBJ_RLD_BYTE		0200
+
+/* Operation codes inside a complex relocation expression (RLD type 017;
+   table 2-7 of the manual).  Codes 0 through 011 -- the arithmetic and
+   the two unary operations -- carry no operand; these are the ones that
+   do, plus the two that end the string.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_LAST_PLAIN	011	/* Up to here: no operand.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_STORE		012	/* Ends the string.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_STORE_DISP	013	/* Ends it, PC-relative.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_GLOBAL		016	/* + 4 bytes of RADIX-50 name.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_PSECT		017	/* + section number and offset.  */
+#define OBJ_CPLX_CONST		020	/* + a 16-bit constant.  */
 
 /* An RLD entry's displacement is measured from its TXT record's type
    word, which is four bytes ahead of that record's data, so the first
@@ -174,6 +185,7 @@
   bfd_byte *contents;
   arelent *relocs;
   unsigned int relcount;	/* Counted, then filled.  */
+  bool unsupported;		/* Carries an RLD entry we cannot use.  */
 };
 
 #define obj_section_data(sec) \
@@ -262,7 +274,51 @@
     case 14: return 6;		/* P-sect additive displaced.  */
     case 16: return 2;		/* Resident library.  */
     default: return -1;
+    }
+}
+
+/* The whole length of a complex relocation entry at AT in a block of
+   SIZE bytes -- its command and displacement bytes plus the string of
+   operation codes that follows them -- or -1 if the string does not end
+   inside the block.  Knowing this is what lets the walk below step over
+   an entry it cannot use instead of giving up on the rest of the
+   block.  */
+
+static int
+obj_pdp11_complex_size (const bfd_byte *body, size_t size, size_t at)
+{
+  size_t k = at + 2;
+
+  while (k < size)
+    {
+      unsigned int op = body[k++];
+
+      switch (op)
+	{
+	case OBJ_CPLX_STORE:
+	case OBJ_CPLX_STORE_DISP:
+	  return (int) (k - at);
+
+	case OBJ_CPLX_GLOBAL:
+	  k += 4;
+	  break;
+
+	case OBJ_CPLX_PSECT:
+	  k += 3;
+	  break;
+
+	case OBJ_CPLX_CONST:
+	  k += 2;
+	  break;
+
+	default:
+	  if (op > OBJ_CPLX_LAST_PLAIN)
+	    return -1;		/* Not an operation code at all.  */
+	  break;
+	}
     }
+
+  return -1;
 }
 
 /* bfd_alloc + strcpy, which libbfd has no spelling for.  */
@@ -634,6 +690,40 @@
    the relocation records, and the relocations themselves -- counted on
    the way through once, then built.  */
 
+/* Say that an RLD entry cannot be turned into a relocation, and mark
+   the section it was meant to modify.  WHAT names the entry and DISP is
+   its displacement byte, which says where in the section the word it
+   would have modified sits.
+
+   The mark is what keeps a bad link from happening quietly: the file
+   stays readable -- its symbols, its contents and its other
+   relocations are all still there to look at -- but asking a marked
+   section for its relocations fails, and that is what every link does
+   (see obj_pdp11_canonicalize_reloc).  */
+
+static void
+obj_pdp11_unsupported (struct obj_pdp11_walk *walk, unsigned int disp,
+		       const char *what)
+{
+  struct obj_pdp11_section_data *data
+    = walk->current != NULL ? obj_section_data (walk->current) : NULL;
+
+  if (data != NULL)
+    data->unsupported = true;
+
+  if (walk->current != NULL && disp >= OBJ_RLD_DISP_BIAS)
+    _bfd_error_handler
+      /* xgettext:c-format */
+      (_("%pB: %pA+%#lx: %s is not supported"),
+       walk->abfd, walk->current,
+       (unsigned long) (walk->txt_offset + disp - OBJ_RLD_DISP_BIAS),
+       what);
+  else
+    _bfd_error_handler
+      /* xgettext:c-format */
+      (_("%pB: %s is not supported"), walk->abfd, what);
+}
+
 static bool
 obj_pdp11_scan_text (struct obj_pdp11_walk *walk, unsigned int type,
 		     const bfd_byte *body, size_t size)
@@ -652,8 +742,48 @@
 	  unsigned int cmd = body[k] & 0x7f;
 	  int arg = obj_pdp11_rld_arg_size (cmd);
 
-	  if (arg < 0 || k + 2 + (size_t) arg > size)
-	    break;
+	  /* A complex expression is a string of operation codes rather
+	     than a fixed argument list, and no ELF relocation can stand
+	     for one: R_PDP11_16 is a symbol plus an addend, with nowhere
+	     to put the arithmetic.  Step over it by its real length, so
+	     that the ordinary entries after it in this block -- which we
+	     can represent perfectly well -- are not lost with it.  */
+	  if (cmd == OBJ_RLD_COMPLEX)
+	    {
+	      int len = obj_pdp11_complex_size (body, size, k);
+
+	      if (walk->counting)
+		obj_pdp11_unsupported (walk, body[k + 1],
+				       _("a complex relocation expression "
+					 "(RLD entry type 017)"));
+	      if (len < 0)
+		break;		/* It does not end in this block.  */
+	      k += (size_t) len;
+	      continue;
+	    }
+
+	  /* An entry type the manual does not define, or one that runs
+	     off the end of its block: either way the next entry's offset
+	     is unknowable, so the walk stops -- but not silently.  */
+	  if (arg < 0)
+	    {
+	      char what[48];
+
+	      /* xgettext:c-format */
+	      snprintf (what, sizeof what, _("RLD entry type 0%o"), cmd);
+	      if (walk->counting)
+		obj_pdp11_unsupported (walk, body[k + 1], what);
+	      break;
+	    }
+
+	  if (k + 2 + (size_t) arg > size)
+	    {
+	      if (walk->counting)
+		obj_pdp11_unsupported (walk, body[k + 1],
+				       _("an RLD entry that runs past the "
+					 "end of its block"));
+	      break;
+	    }
 
 	  if (cmd == OBJ_RLD_LOCCTR_DEF)
 	    {
@@ -914,13 +1044,26 @@
 }
 
 static long
-obj_pdp11_canonicalize_reloc (bfd *abfd ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED, asection *sec,
+obj_pdp11_canonicalize_reloc (bfd *abfd, asection *sec,
 			      arelent **relptr,
 			      asymbol **symbols ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED)
 {
   struct obj_pdp11_section_data *data = obj_section_data (sec);
   unsigned int i;
 
+  /* Reading the file was fine; using these relocations is not.  What
+     was skipped was already reported, by kind and by place, when the
+     file was read -- see obj_pdp11_unsupported.  */
+  if (data != NULL && data->unsupported)
+    {
+      _bfd_error_handler
+	/* xgettext:c-format */
+	(_("%pB: %pA needs relocations this reader cannot represent"),
+	 abfd, sec);
+      bfd_set_error (bfd_error_invalid_operation);
+      return -1;
+    }
+
   if (data == NULL || data->relocs == NULL)
     {
       *relptr = NULL;
